WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.190
- Hey, LegalEagles.

00:00:01.190 --> 00:00:02.860
It's true, a little while ago,

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:05.610
this YouTube channel did in fact file suit

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.320
against the White House, the CIA, the DOJ,

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.470
the Department of State,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.120
and a whole bunch of
other government agencies.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.500
On Twitter and YouTube,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.200
I asked for the questions that you had

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.300
about this particular lawsuit
and you did not disappoint.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.130
I got over 2000 questions
about this ongoing suit.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.990
And since this is a great opportunity

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.750
to take you guys along for the ride,

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.160
I thought I would try and answer some

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.310
of the most common questions that you had

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.530
about this lawsuit.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:31.830
Now I'll start off by answering one

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.050
of the most common questions,

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.160
which is why?

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.400
And I think that the point of this lawsuit

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.490
was misunderstood by a lot of people.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.010
The point of this lawsuit is
governmental transparency,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.060
which I dare to say I think most people

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.070
should be in favor of more
governmental transparency.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.620
Especially into a part
of government review

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.940
that is not particularly well known,

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.200
and is famously opaque.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.210
And that is the
prepublication review process

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:57.760
for people that have
classified information

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.380
and then want to write a book.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.020
And famously, this is
something that governments

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.330
don't want people to know very much about.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:06.680
And under the circumstances,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.590
it really looks like there
were some political shenanigans

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.850
that got in the way of
John Bolton being able

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:15.240
to publish one or more
different manuscripts

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.280
of his book for political reasons,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.760
not for national security reasons.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.090
And a suit like this only works

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.490
when the government does
something that it shouldn't,

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.070
that it prevents a former
government official

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.740
from being able to disclose information

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.290
that he or she should be able to disclose.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.240
If the government had upheld the standards

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.000
of prepublication review,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.470
the lawsuit wouldn't have anywhere to go.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.710
Because everything would have
been properly classified.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:42.830
Given what we know,

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.840
it seems that that is incredibly unlikely,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.950
and that some things were
prohibited from being disclosed

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.320
that really shouldn't have been.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.060
And a lot of people also misunderstood

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.240
what we are trying to accomplish here.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.580
Which is that remember,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.140
John Bolton submitted
an original manuscript

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.500
of his book to the government,

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.140
then they went through
a whole process saying

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.710
what he could and could not publish.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.150
And then they said he was okay to publish,

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.150
and then reneged on that agreement

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.020
when a political appointee came in

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.240
after the career administrator
said that the book

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.670
was clear to go.

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:15.870
So the point of the FOIA requests

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.430
is not seeking the book that
has already been published,

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.080
but going towards the original book

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.110
that John Bolton wanted to publish,

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.200
even before the initial
prepublication review.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.033
And on top of that,

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.440
we're also looking for the communications

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:31.870
amongst the government officials,

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.370
in terms of what considerations
were they thinking about

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.860
when they said John Bolton could

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.350
and could not publish things.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.650
Is their correspondence
with Michael Ellis,

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.380
the political appointee,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.530
saying that John Bolton
couldn't publish his book

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.130
for political reasons?

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.740
And then of course,
there's the question of

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.690
what standards if any
did they use to decide

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.500
whether John Bolton can publish
the information in his book?

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.450
So we're seeking a lot of
different kinds of documents

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.400
and not just the book that was published?

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.240
But the original book that
John Bolton wanted to publish.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.810
And the correspondence

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:06.960
about the prepublication review process.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.280
And what standards if any they were using.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.260
And even if the government
didn't disclose anything

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:14.400
other than explaining
who did the redactions

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.800
of John Bolton's book,

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.510
even that would be incredibly
important information

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.260
to get a glimpse into
whether the redactions

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.220
were correct or not.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.053
And on top of that,

00:03:25.053 --> 00:03:27.070
when the US government
responds to a FOIA request,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.500
they have to provide way more specificity

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.020
than they do when they're conducting

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.620
the prepublication review process.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.840
In pre-pub review, they
can basically say remove

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.870
this information and not
really give you a reason why.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.430
When responding to FOIA,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.010
they have to tell us if they
were removing information

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.980
because it was classified
or for some other reason.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.540
So even getting those
responses would be a win,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.610
because if they told John Bolton

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.780
that they were removing things
because it was classified.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.800
And then tell us and their FOIA requests

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.770
that the information wasn't classified.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:03:58.760
Well, then we know that
there were real shenanigans

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.450
going on in the
prepublication review process

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.760
in a way that absolutely
should not happen.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:08.580
And this particular suit
on behalf of LegalEagle,

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.740
it's not entirely new.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.480
We're standing on the
shoulders of some real giants

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.090
in the world of FOIA and
governmental transparency.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:19.590
Organizations like CREW, MuckRock,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.440
Judicial Watch back in the day.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.970
All of these organizations

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:25.810
are the real unsung heroes of FOIA,

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.510
and are really the ones
that are working every day

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.430
to disclose things that the US government

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:33.220
doesn't want people to see.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.620
So we are following in the trail

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.160
that they have already blazed.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.480
And we're indebted to
those kind of organizations

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.240
that are working every day

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.570
to further governmental transparency.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.600
Now, this particular lawsuit is different

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.920
for some reasons that we will talk about,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.200
and there's some interesting
implications of it.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:51.170
But with that being said,

00:04:51.170 --> 00:04:54.360
let's dive into all of
your particular questions

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.510
about this lawsuit.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:58.110
Okay, so one of the most
popular questions I got

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.260
is a variation of this
question that was asked

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:02.980
by user Agma Schwa, who asks.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.130
How much does it cost to sue
something like the White House?

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.860
Now, when you're talking about lawsuits,

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.000
there are generally two big
things that cost the most.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.220
And they're divided into costs and fees.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.210
Costs are things like how much
it costs to file the lawsuit.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.100
How much it costs to do the
various transactional things

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.670
like have a court reporter.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.260
And then there are the
the much bigger things

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:25.100
that are called fees.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.280
And that generally refers
to how much it costs

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:28.680
for your lawyer.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.110
And in the absence of
a fee shifting statute,

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.880
You gotta pay for your own lawyer.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:34.940
In this particular case,

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:37.760
it costs $400 to file a federal lawsuit.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.590
That's pretty standard.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:41.560
Then it'll cost probably
a few thousand dollars

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.950
in terms of other filing fees

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.940
and things related to court reporters

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:49.970
and stenographers for
depositions if we get there.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.810
Which for reasons we won't
go into, we probably won't.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:56.440
So on the order in terms
of the actual raw fees

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.510
you're talking several thousand dollars.

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:00.210
Then when you're talking about attorneys,

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:01.730
believe it or not in Washington DC

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.430
probably an average cost of
an attorney is $500 an hour.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.460
They're really, really
good big firm attorneys,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.910
you're probably talking about
1000, 1200 dollars an hour.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.010
So for the kind of things
that you're talking about

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.380
in this litigation it's
gonna cost definitely

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.510
a few hundred hours of attorney time.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.900
So you guys can do the math on that.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.620
So obviously, the big X factor

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.970
is how many hours of the attorneys time

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.100
are you going to need to be able

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.710
to propound something like this.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:30.100
And you know, on the very minimum,

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:31.710
you're talking tens of
thousands of dollars

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:32.543
of attorney time,

00:06:32.543 --> 00:06:34.510
and it could be hundreds
of thousands of dollars

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:35.530
of attorney time if you're talking

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:37.020
about taking this on appeal.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.630
Which is particularly
likely in a case like this.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.190
So yeah, lawyers and
lawsuits, very expensive.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.530
Which takes us to the next question

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.250
by a user named his Ispirov Junior.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:49.600
Question, have any suits

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.500
against the White House
succeeded in the past,

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.400
and if so, how frequently
does this happen?

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.270
So actually, I can point you
to one very recent example

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:01.840
of pretty similar lawsuits
that was very, very successful.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:04.260
And there is a journalist at BuzzFeed

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.980
whose name is Jason Leopold,

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:09.300
who has done a lot of great stuff related

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:10.560
to the Mueller report.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.180
And recently, Mr. Leopold
filed a FOIA request

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.070
and then a FOIA lawsuit to
enforce his FOIA requests.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:21.480
To try and get the
unredacted Mueller report.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.120
And as a result, lots of the portions

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.010
of the original Mueller
report were revealed.

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:30.340
And further portions of the Mueller report

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.030
were shown with more specificity

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:34.290
as to why they were
redacted and not revealed.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.760
So that is an example of
a successful similar suit

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:41.090
that as a result of Jason
Leopold and BuzzFeed,

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:43.750
we now know a lot more about
the original Mueller report.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:44.880
We don't know everything,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.380
but we do know that large
portions were redacted

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:48.690
when they probably shouldn't have been.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.470
And he's gonna continue we'll probably get

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:52.600
even more portions of the Mueller report.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:56.090
And in terms of successful and
how often they're successful.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:57.390
Well, it depends on on what you mean.

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.180
In terms of getting some of the previous

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.760
redacted information and
getting more specificity

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:05.410
as to why other things were
redacted in the first place,

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:06.940
happens pretty frequently.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.040
It's rare that you get all of
the documents that you want.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.740
But in terms of holding the
government's feet to the fire

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:14.270
and getting more information,

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:18.010
which is part of the reason
for this particular suit,

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:19.730
it happens with some regularity.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.530
Okay, user JLdaydream asks,

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:23.740
since you've sued the White House,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.590
are you actually allowed
to comment on this

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.190
as it's now an ongoing case?

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:28.583
That is a very good question.

00:08:28.583 --> 00:08:30.770
As we've talked about
a lot on this channel,

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:33.260
it's really, really bad
idea for most people

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.070
to go out and talk about
ongoing litigation.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:37.283
That being said,

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.095
this video will be approved.

00:08:40.095 --> 00:08:41.450
(beep)

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:42.920
So the odds of me saying something

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:44.970
that are incredibly prejudicial to my case

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.290
are probably pretty low.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:51.160
But this is an example of
do as I say, not as I do.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.500
If you find yourself in
litigation or lawsuit

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.720
or in trouble with the government,

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.700
best to let your lawyers
handle everything.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.360
But also by the same token,

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.530
don't take legal advice

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:02.286
from a YouTube channel.

00:09:02.286 --> 00:09:03.960
#notlegaladvice.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.730
Alright, user tragic solitude asks,

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:08.340
how difficult is it to sue something

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.920
as big as entire departments
of the government?

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.500
Well, it's not the easiest
thing in the world.

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.050
Alright, so user justApoet asks,

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.790
so is this the same as suing
the United States government,

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:22.940
Or is the White House in this
context and entity of its own?

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:24.050
That's a great question.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:26.620
We are suing several different agencies

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.660
and entities within the government.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:30.120
So when I say the White House,

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.240
I'm actually referring to a
couple of different departments.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:38.170
And primarily, we are suing
the National Security Council,

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.970
which John Bolton was the head of.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:42.680
He was the national security adviser.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.280
That is one of the large portions.

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.890
We're also suing other
agencies within the government

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:49.550
that probably had access
to John Bolton's book,

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:50.740
and might have participated

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:52.587
in the prepublication review process.

00:09:52.587 --> 00:09:55.550
And so that's why we're
suing entities like the CIA,

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:56.680
and the Department of Justice,

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.280
and a whole bunch of other entities.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:00.990
So I refer to that generally
as the white house,

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:04.070
but we're actually suing a
bunch of different entities

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.500
all because they play different roles

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:09.270
with respect to this scenario.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:12.330
Okay, next question is 1 SwedishFish.

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.250
Ah, too bad you only
have one Swedish fish,

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:15.220
Swedish fish are great.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.810
How long do you think this
whole ordeal will last?

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:20.430
So this lawsuit is going to
take several different forms.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.210
And over time, different
things will be adjudicated.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.810
And the first thing that we have to do

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.940
is make sure that
LegalEagle's FOIA requests,

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.260
the original ones are entitled

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.020
to what's called expedited review.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:34.130
Because for among other things,

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.670
this information about John Bolton's book

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:38.470
and the process that happened

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:40.930
and the information that
he was trying to divulge

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:41.890
are really important.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:43.770
It was presumably part and parcel

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.950
to the whole impeachment saga,

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:48.640
and it could be very well relevant

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:50.780
to the election in November.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:55.470
So the FOIA requests themselves
need to be adjudicated,

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:56.590
to be expedited.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.890
So that they can be processed
in a much quicker way.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:03.180
So in terms of getting a court to order

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.530
that these requests are indeed related

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:06.600
to a compelling interest

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.420
